For use with the Slurm system on HX2, Imperial College London's HPC cluster.

Tested with GCHP 14.7.1 (fullchem; C24 and C90; 1 to 8 nodes) in October
2026, using the modules in gchp.env (GCC 13.3.0, Open MPI 5.0.3, ESMF 8.7.0)
and also foss-2025b (GCC 14.3.0, Open MPI 5.0.8, ESMF 8.9.0).

Setup:

Build GCHP with gchp.env loaded. In your run directory, point gchp.env to
this file and copy gchp.run:

./setEnvironmentLink.sh /path/to/icl_hx2/gchp.env
cp /path/to/icl_hx2/gchp.run .

To submit a job, type:

sbatch gchp.run

Instructions are given to modify node/core/memory requirements in gchp.run.
Set TOTAL_CORES, NUM_NODES and NUM_CORES_PER_NODE in setCommonRunSettings.sh
to match --nodes and --ntasks-per-node.

Open MPI 5 setting:

gchp.run sets OMPI_MCA_coll=^han. Without it, GCHP crashes at the first
time step on 3 or more nodes, and results depend on how the cores are
spread over nodes. Please keep this line, also for 1- and 2-node runs.
It was needed with Open MPI 5.0.3 and 5.0.8 on HX2. The crash also
depends on the memory allocator (it did not occur with jemalloc
preloaded), but turning HAN off needs no extra library.
See https://github.com/geoschem/GCHP/issues/578

Choosing nodes and cores:

- HX2 nodes have 144 cores; a job can use up to 142 per node. A job can use
  at most 8 nodes, and one user can use at most 1040 cores at a time. The
  longest wall time is 3 days. (Limits in October 2026.)
- Single-node jobs are the most efficient per core, and fewer processes
  per node are faster per core. If you run many simulations, many small
  single-node jobs (for example C24 with 30 to 60 cores) give the most
  model years per day within the per-user core limit.
- More nodes are worth it only when one simulation must finish quickly.
  At C90, use 1 or 2 nodes. On 4 or more nodes the speed varied a lot
  between jobs, and 8 nodes were slower than 2 nodes because of
  communication between nodes (cause not known).
- Measured speed (fullchem, model days per wall-clock day, one 1-day run
  each, shared nodes, about +/-15%):
    C24: 1 x 30 cores: 202    1 x 60: 357    1 x 96: 492    2 x 60: 626
    C90: 1 x 96 cores: 38     2 x 96: 71     2 x 120: 77    4 x 120: 134
         8 x 120: about 50
- Memory: peak use was at most 1.8 GB per process at C24 and 2.8 GB per
  process at C90. Requests that worked: 150 GB per node for up to 60
  processes at C24, and 400 GB per node for up to 120 processes at C90.
- With OMPI_MCA_coll=^han, runs with the same total number of cores give
  bit-for-bit identical results, whatever the number of nodes. Changing
  the total number of cores changes results at round-off level, so use the
  same core count for simulations you want to compare.

Known issue:

GCHP 14.7.1 aborts at exit on HX2 (SIGABRT, "malloc_consolidate(): invalid
chunk size"), on 1 node too, after all output and restart files are
written. It does not affect results: gchp.run checks cap_restart, renames
the new restart file, and the job ends as COMPLETED. The GCHP developers
confirmed that it is harmless, and it is fixed for GCHP 14.8.1
(https://github.com/geoschem/GCHP/issues/576).

